D={
"Mansa Musa":("Mansa Musa (r. about 1312–1337) ruled the Mali Empire at its height, controlling gold fields that supplied much of the Old World's gold. His pilgrimage to Mecca in 1324, with thousands of attendants and camels carrying gold, made him famous; his spending in Cairo depressed gold prices for years. He returned with scholars and the architect Es-Saheli, building the Djinguereber mosque and making Timbuktu a centre of Islamic learning. The 1375 Catalan Atlas shows him holding a gold nugget.","Mali Empire; Keita dynasty (Mali); Mansa Musa's hajj; Islam; Timbuktu manuscripts"),
"Krishnadevaraya":("Krishnadevaraya (r. 1509–1529) of the Tuluva dynasty was Vijayanagara's greatest emperor. He defeated the Bijapur Sultanate at Raichur (1520), took territories from the Gajapatis of Odisha and kept Portuguese friendship for horses. He built gopurams at Hampi and Tirupati, and his court hosted the 'Ashtadiggajas', eight great Telugu poets, as well as Tenali Rama of folk tales. He wrote Amuktamalyada, a Telugu poem on statecraft and devotion.","Vijayanagara Empire; Sangama, Saluva and Tuluva dynasties (Vijayanagara); Battle of Talikota; Hinduism; Bhakti movement"),
"Shah Jahan":("Shah Jahan (r. 1628–1658), the fifth Mughal emperor, presided over the empire's architectural golden age. He built the Taj Mahal (1632–1653) in Agra as a tomb for his wife Mumtaz Mahal, who died giving birth to their 14th child; the new capital Shahjahanabad with the Red Fort and Jama Masjid in Delhi; and the jewelled Peacock Throne. Costly wars and building strained finances. His son Aurangzeb defeated his brothers and imprisoned Shah Jahan in Agra Fort, where he died in 1666 looking at the Taj.","Mughal Empire; Mughal dynasty; Akbar; Babur; Death of Aurangzeb; Islam; Nadir Shah sacks Delhi"),
"Chhatrapati Shivaji":("Shivaji Bhonsle (1630–1680) grew up in Pune under his mother Jijabai and began seizing forts from the Bijapur Sultanate as a teenager. He killed the Bijapur general Afzal Khan at Pratapgad (1659), surprised Mughal governor Shaista Khan in Pune (1663), raided Surat, and escaped from Aurangzeb's house arrest in Agra (1666) reportedly hidden in fruit baskets. He was crowned Chhatrapati at Raigad in 1674, set up the Ashta Pradhan council, a navy and a revenue system, and promoted Marathi and Sanskrit in administration.","Maratha Confederacy; Maratha Empire (Bhonsle and Peshwas); Shivaji's coronation; Mughal Empire; Death of Aurangzeb; Hinduism"),
"Peter the Great":("Peter I (1672–1725) became tsar as a boy and travelled incognito to the Netherlands and England (1697–98), working in shipyards. Determined to modernise Russia, he built a navy, reorganised the army, forced nobles to shave beards and wear European clothes, and created the Table of Ranks based on service. He defeated Sweden in the Great Northern War (Poltava, 1709), founded St Petersburg as a 'window on Europe', and declared Russia an empire in 1721. He was brutal: thousands of serfs died building his capital, and he had his own son tortured to death.","Russian Empire; Romanov dynasty; Christianity"),
"Maharaja Ranjit Singh":("Ranjit Singh (1780–1839), who lost an eye to smallpox as a child, became chief of the Sukerchakia misl and captured Lahore in 1799, uniting the Sikh misls into an empire covering Punjab, Kashmir, Multan and Peshawar. He modernised his Khalsa army with European officers, ruled with ministers of all faiths, and gilded the Harmandir Sahib (Golden Temple). He owned the Koh-i-Noor diamond. After his death, intrigues weakened the state and the British annexed Punjab in 1849.","Sikh Empire; Sikh Empire (Sukerchakia); Sikhism; British Raj; East India Company rule"),
"Tipu Sultan":("Tipu Sultan (1750–1799), son of Hyder Ali, ruled Mysore from 1782. Called the 'Tiger of Mysore', he fought four wars against the East India Company, using iron-cased rockets that later inspired Britain's Congreve rockets. He introduced a new calendar, coins and silk farming, and sought alliances with Napoleon and the Ottomans. He is admired as an anti-colonial fighter but criticised for violence against Kodavas, Malabar Hindus and Christians. He died defending Srirangapatna in 1799.","Kingdom of Mysore; Mysore (Wodeyar and Hyder Ali); Fall of Srirangapatna; First Anglo-Mysore War to Fourth; Napoleon Bonaparte; Islam"),
"Confucius":("Kong Qiu (551–479 BCE), known as Kongzi or Confucius, was a minor official in the state of Lu during the Spring and Autumn period. He taught that society is harmonious when people follow ritual (li), practise humaneness (ren), honour their parents (filial piety) and rulers govern by virtue rather than force. He travelled among states seeking a ruler to apply his ideas, without success, and taught many students who compiled his sayings in the Analects. Under the Han dynasty Confucianism became the state ideology, shaping East Asia for 2,000 years.","Confucianism; Zhou dynasty; Han dynasty; Laozi; Axial Age"),
"Laozi":("Laozi ('Old Master') is the traditional author of the Daodejing, a short book of 81 poetic chapters on the Dao ('the Way'), the natural order of the universe. Legend says he was a Zhou archivist who, leaving China on a water buffalo, wrote the book for a border guard. It teaches simplicity, humility and wu wei (effortless action). Historians debate whether he was a real person. He is honoured as the founder of Taoism.","Taoism; Zhou dynasty; Confucius; Guodian bamboo slips"),
"Gautama Buddha":("Siddhartha Gautama was born in Lumbini (Nepal), a prince of the Shakya republic, probably in the 5th century BCE. Moved by seeing old age, sickness and death, he left home at 29, tried severe asceticism, then found the 'Middle Way' and attained enlightenment under a pipal tree at Bodh Gaya. He preached his first sermon at Sarnath on the Four Noble Truths and the Eightfold Path, founded the sangha including nuns, and taught for 45 years under the patronage of kings like Bimbisara. He died at Kushinagar aged 80.","Buddhism; Buddha's enlightenment; Magadha; Bimbisara; Mahavira; Axial Age; Ashoka"),
"Mahavira":("Vardhamana Mahavira (traditionally 599–527 BCE; many scholars place him in the 5th century BCE) was born near Vaishali into a kshatriya family. At 30 he renounced his home, practised austerities for 12 years and attained kevala jnana (omniscience). He taught the five vows — non-violence (ahimsa), truth, non-stealing, chastity and non-attachment — and organised monks, nuns and laity. He is the 24th tirthankara of Jainism and attained nirvana at Pawapuri.","Jainism; Magadha; Gautama Buddha; Axial Age; Chandragupta Maurya"),
"Socrates":("Socrates (about 470–399 BCE) was an Athenian stonemason's son who served as a hoplite in the Peloponnesian War. He wrote nothing; we know him through Plato and Xenophon. He questioned people about justice, courage and virtue (the Socratic method), claiming wisdom lay in knowing that he knew nothing. Accused of impiety and corrupting youth, he was sentenced to death by drinking hemlock in 399 BCE, refusing to escape.","Classical Greece; Death of Socrates; Plato; Aristotle; Peloponnesian War; Athenian democracy"),
"Plato":("Plato (about 428–348 BCE), Socrates's student, founded the Academy in Athens, which lasted centuries. His dialogues, such as the Republic, Symposium and Phaedo, discuss justice, love, the soul and his theory of Forms — that the physical world is a shadow of eternal ideas (the Allegory of the Cave). The Republic imagined a state ruled by philosopher-kings. Alfred North Whitehead said Western philosophy is 'a series of footnotes to Plato'.","Classical Greece; Socrates; Aristotle; Greek religion and mythology; Axial Age"),
"Aristotle":("Aristotle (384–322 BCE), from Stagira, studied at Plato's Academy for 20 years, tutored the young Alexander the Great, and founded the Lyceum in Athens. He wrote on logic, biology, physics, ethics, politics, poetry and rhetoric, classifying animals and developing syllogistic logic. His works were preserved by Islamic scholars and dominated European thought in the Middle Ages.","Classical Greece; Plato; Socrates; Alexander the Great; Macedonian Empire; Ibn Sina (Avicenna)"),
"Chanakya":("Chanakya, also called Kautilya or Vishnugupta, was a Brahmin scholar, traditionally from Taxila, who helped Chandragupta Maurya overthrow the Nanda dynasty around 322 BCE and served as his chief minister. He is credited with the Arthashastra, a treatise on statecraft, economics, law, espionage and war, rediscovered in 1905. His realism has led to comparisons with Machiavelli. Legends also attribute aphorisms (Chanakya Niti) to him.","Maurya Empire; Chandragupta Maurya; Nanda Empire; Arthashastra; Magadha"),
"Panini":("Panini, a grammarian from Shalatula near Gandhara (around the 5th–4th century BCE), wrote the Ashtadhyayi, about 4,000 concise rules (sutras) describing Sanskrit's sounds, word formation and syntax. His system uses meta-rules, recursion and abbreviations similar to modern formal languages; the Backus–Naur form used in computer science is sometimes called Panini–Backus form. His grammar standardised Classical Sanskrit.","Classical Sanskrit; Vedic Sanskrit; Brahmi; Axial Age"),
"Archimedes":("Archimedes (about 287–212 BCE) of Syracuse in Sicily was the greatest mathematician of antiquity. He approximated π, calculated areas and volumes using methods close to integral calculus, discovered the principle of buoyancy (reportedly shouting 'Eureka!' in his bath), and explained levers ('Give me a place to stand and I will move the Earth'). He designed war machines to defend Syracuse against Rome and was killed by a Roman soldier during the city's fall.","Classical Greece; Punic Wars; Archimedes Palimpsest; Euclid"),
"Sushruta":("Sushruta, a physician traditionally said to have taught at Kashi (Varanasi) around the 6th century BCE (the text took shape over centuries), is credited with the Sushruta Samhita. It describes over 300 surgical procedures and 120 instruments, including rhinoplasty using a cheek or forehead flap, cataract surgery and treatment of fractures, and recommends practising on gourds and dead animals. He is called the 'father of surgery'.","Hinduism; Charaka; Vedic India; Gupta Empire"),
"Charaka":("Charaka, said to have been a court physician of the Kushan king Kanishka, edited the Charaka Samhita, a foundational text of Ayurveda. It explains health as a balance of three doshas (vata, pitta, kapha), describes diagnosis, diet, herbal medicines and preventive care, and contains an oath for physicians. It was translated into Persian and Arabic.","Kushan Empire; Kanishka; Sushruta; Hinduism"),
"Thiruvalluvar":("Thiruvalluvar, a Tamil poet-philosopher of uncertain date (perhaps 1st century BCE–5th century CE), wrote the Tirukkural, 1,330 couplets in three books on virtue (aram), wealth and politics (porul) and love (inbam). Its universal ethics, free of sectarian references, made it one of the most translated Indian works. A 133-foot statue of him stands at Kanyakumari.","Sangam-era Tamilakam; Old Tamil; Jainism; Hinduism"),
"Aryabhata":("Aryabhata (476–550 CE) wrote the Aryabhatiya at age 23 in Kusumapura (Pataliputra) during the Gupta era. He used place-value notation, approximated π as 3.1416, gave sine tables, and proposed that the Earth rotates on its axis, explaining the apparent motion of the stars. He explained solar and lunar eclipses through shadows rather than the demon Rahu. India's first satellite (1975) was named after him.","Gupta Empire; Brahmagupta; Bhaskara II; Al-Khwarizmi; Nalanda founded"),
"Kalidasa":("Kalidasa, probably of the 4th–5th century CE and linked with the Gupta court of Chandragupta II (Vikramaditya), is the greatest poet and playwright of classical Sanskrit. His play Abhijnanashakuntalam, about King Dushyanta and Shakuntala, enchanted Goethe when translated in 1789. His poems Meghaduta (a cloud carries a message to a lover) and Raghuvamsha are masterpieces of nature imagery.","Gupta Empire; Classical Sanskrit; Hinduism; Samudragupta"),
"Brahmagupta":("Brahmagupta (598–668), from Bhinmal in Rajasthan, wrote the Brahmasphutasiddhanta (628), the first text to treat zero as a number with rules for arithmetic: a number minus itself is zero; negative times negative is positive. He solved quadratic equations and gave Brahmagupta's formula for cyclic quadrilaterals. His work, translated into Arabic in Baghdad around 771, shaped Islamic and later European mathematics.","Aryabhata; Al-Khwarizmi; Founding of Baghdad and the House of Wisdom; Gurjara-Pratihara dynasty"),
"Adi Shankaracharya":("Adi Shankara (traditionally 788–820, though some date him earlier) was born at Kaladi in Kerala and became a renunciant as a boy. He wrote commentaries on the Upanishads, Bhagavad Gita and Brahma Sutras, teaching Advaita Vedanta: the self (atman) is identical with Brahman, and the world is maya. He travelled across India debating rival schools and founded four mathas at Sringeri, Dwarka, Puri and Jyotirmath.","Hinduism; Vedic religion; Buddhism; Upanishads"),
"Al-Khwarizmi":("Muhammad ibn Musa al-Khwarizmi (about 780–850), a Persian scholar at Baghdad's House of Wisdom, wrote al-Kitab al-Mukhtasar fi Hisab al-Jabr wal-Muqabala, from which 'algebra' comes. His book on Indian numerals introduced decimal place-value arithmetic to the Islamic world and, when translated into Latin, to Europe; the word 'algorithm' comes from the Latin form of his name. He also worked on astronomy and geography.","Abbasid Caliphate; Founding of Baghdad and the House of Wisdom; Brahmagupta; Al-Khwarizmi's Algebra; Islam"),
}
